# Dependencies
node_modules/
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
# package-lock.json is deliberately NOT ignored. The deploy image is built on a
# runner that is not this machine, and two of these trees compile native code
# (bcrypt in userapi, the web3/keccak stack in walletapi). Resolving dependencies
# fresh on every build means someone else's transitive release can break the deploy
# with no change on our side. Lockfiles committed, `npm ci` in the Dockerfile.
yarn.lock
pnpm-lock.yaml

# Environment variables
#
# THIS SECTION USED TO LIST ONLY `.env` AND `.env.local`, AND THE SERVICES DO
# NOT USE EITHER NAME. Each service is started with
# `dotenv_config_path=local.env` (the frontend with `env-cmd -f local.env`), so
# the files that actually hold configuration are `local.env`, `dev.env` and
# `prod.env` - none of which matched any pattern here. All three are therefore
# committed, in every service, with live-looking API keys in them.
#
# `*.env` closes that. Note what it does NOT do: a pattern only stops git
# tracking a file it is not ALREADY tracking. The env files already in the index
# stay in the index until someone runs
#
#     git rm --cached <service>/{local,dev,prod}.env
#
# which removes them from git and LEAVES THEM ON DISK, so the running stack is
# unaffected. That, plus rotating the keys, is the owner's call and is written
# up in the root README under "Configuration and secrets".
#
# `*.env.example` and `.env.local.example` are the committed templates a clone
# copies from, and are excluded from the exclusion.
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local
*.env
# `.env.*` catches the framework-conventional names - .env.production,
# .env.development, .env.test, .env.staging - which `*.env` does not match
# because the extension comes first. Only the frontend's own .gitignore had
# this; a `.env.production` dropped in any backend would have been committable.
.env.*
# Editor and hand-made backups. `*.env` does not match `local.env.bak`, and a
# `cryptodex-spotapi/local.env.bak` really was committed
# once - it is still in the history.
*.env.bak
*.env.backup
*.env.orig
*.env.save
*.env.old
# These negations must stay LAST: `.env.*` above would otherwise re-ignore the
# committed templates.
!*.env.example
!.env.example
!.env.local.example
!.env.template
!.env.webhook.template

